102
00:06:01,060 --> 00:06:04,600
and to talk a lot today
about Max entropy inverse

103
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:05,950
reinforcement learning.

104
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:08,670
So let's just remember
where we were last time.

105
00:06:08,670 --> 00:06:11,710
What were talking about when we
talked about imitation learning

106
00:06:11,710 --> 00:06:15,080
was the idea of taking
demonstrations from people.

107
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:17,530
And these either could be
explicit demonstrations.

108
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:20,920
Like, I show the robot
how to pick up a cup,

109
00:06:20,920 --> 00:06:23,290
and it records all my
movements, and then

110
00:06:23,290 --> 00:06:25,720
you can use that
for later training;

111
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:28,250
or it could just be
natural trajectories.

112
00:06:28,250 --> 00:06:30,453
So you take electronic
medical record systems,

113
00:06:30,453 --> 00:06:31,870
and you just look
at the decisions

114
00:06:31,870 --> 00:06:33,770
that are made from doctors.

115
00:06:33,770 --> 00:06:37,300
And we use that to try to
either equal doctor performance

116
00:06:37,300 --> 00:06:39,190
or exceed doctor performance.

117
00:06:39,190 --> 00:06:41,420
So often, we just
have observation data,

118
00:06:41,420 --> 00:06:43,790
which may either just be done
in normal sort of business

119
00:06:43,790 --> 00:06:47,490
as usual, or that is explicitly
being given as a demonstration

120
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:48,480
trajectory.

121
00:06:48,480 --> 00:06:52,000
And this is just going to be the
sequence of states and actions.

122
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,230
We're not going to have
rewards in general.

123
00:06:55,230 --> 00:06:59,280
And so the idea was, well, it
might be easier in some cases

124
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:02,340
either because it's just
sort of natural data traces

125
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:04,930
that are being generated as
part of their normal work,

126
00:07:04,930 --> 00:07:08,250
like, electronic medical
record systems are;

127
00:07:08,250 --> 00:07:12,090
or because it's hard
for people to write down

128
00:07:12,090 --> 00:07:13,982
a reward function
that kind of captures

129
00:07:13,982 --> 00:07:15,690
all the complexity of
what they're trying

130
00:07:15,690 --> 00:07:17,820
to do in their objective.

131
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:20,670
So that was one of the
motivations for this.

132
00:07:20,670 --> 00:07:22,740
And we saw a few
different ways to try

133
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:25,430
to think about this setting
last time, including

134
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:27,180
behavior cloning, where
we just map things

135
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:28,830
back to supervised learning.

136
00:07:28,830 --> 00:07:32,790
And we try to learn a policy
directly to match the expert.

137
00:07:32,790 --> 00:07:34,875
We saw DAGGER.

138
00:07:34,875 --> 00:07:36,560
I'll put that on here, too.

139
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:39,150
So another thing that we saw
kind of in between these two

140
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:44,250
was DAGGER, which tried to
address a challenge of behavior

141
00:07:44,250 --> 00:07:47,850
cloning, which is that
when you make mistakes

142
00:07:47,850 --> 00:07:49,780
in your supervised
learning system,

143
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:53,430
you may end up in parts of the
state and action distribution

144
00:07:53,430 --> 00:07:54,392
that you don't know.

145
00:07:54,392 --> 00:07:55,600
You don't have good coverage.

146
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:59,580
So we talked about this kind
of race car track example

147
00:07:59,580 --> 00:08:03,630
where once you go off, you've
got a distribution mismatch.

148
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:06,960
And we'll hear more about
distribution mismatches in RL

149
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,220
later in the course.

150
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:10,420
And there we wouldn't
necessarily know what to do.

151
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:13,800
And so what DAGGER said is we
have to keep an expert around,

152
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:16,360
and then they will always tell
us what you should have done.

153
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:17,818
So they're kind of
a coach go back.

154
00:08:17,818 --> 00:08:20,290
They replay how you did
in that hockey game;

155
00:08:20,290 --> 00:08:22,378
what you should have
done at each moment.

156
00:08:22,378 --> 00:08:24,420
And there's a lot of really
interesting questions

157
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:27,238
of thinking about
those counterfactuals.

158
00:08:27,238 --> 00:08:29,530
And then we thought about
this broad question of, well,

159
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:34,830
could we recover the reward from
looking at these demonstrations?

160
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:37,830
And this could be useful
within its own right

161
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:40,113
to try to understand
the objectives

162
00:08:40,113 --> 00:08:41,530
that people are
using when they're

163
00:08:41,530 --> 00:08:45,040
making their decisions
for different areas,

164
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:47,500
as well as potentially for
learning a better policy

165
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:49,390
or learning the policy.

166
00:08:49,390 --> 00:08:51,310
And then can we also--

167
00:08:51,310 --> 00:08:53,920
one we have that r--
generate a good policy

168
00:08:53,920 --> 00:08:57,600
or generate a good
policy directly?

169
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:00,870
So one of the ideas that we
talked about in this case

170
00:09:00,870 --> 00:09:06,690
is, well, what is sufficient to
be able to accomplish mimicking?

171
00:09:06,690 --> 00:09:09,330
So in particular,
we said, well, if we

172
00:09:09,330 --> 00:09:13,320
want to get a policy that
matches the expert, that

173
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:16,950
is equivalent to generating
trajectories, where

174
00:09:16,950 --> 00:09:18,750
that distribution over
those trajectories

175
00:09:18,750 --> 00:09:21,660
is the same as what the
expert would have done.

176
00:09:21,660 --> 00:09:27,660
So we think of this strong
relationship between policies

177
00:09:27,660 --> 00:09:34,660
to trajectories, which also
is to states and actions

178
00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:37,120
because we can
think of there being

179
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:40,030
a policy that induces a
distribution over states

180
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:40,990
and actions.

181
00:09:40,990 --> 00:09:44,530
And two policies that
induce the same distribution

182
00:09:44,530 --> 00:09:47,420
over states and actions
will have the same reward

183
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:49,420
because we're assuming
that the rewards are only

184
00:09:49,420 --> 00:09:52,180
a function of the
states and actions.

185
00:09:52,180 --> 00:09:54,520
And so we talked
about how people

186
00:09:54,520 --> 00:09:56,380
had sort of leveraged
this assumption

187
00:09:56,380 --> 00:10:00,740
to think about different ways
to try to learn reward features.

188
00:10:00,740 --> 00:10:03,340
So, for example, if you
have a set of features

189
00:10:03,340 --> 00:10:04,910
to buy your policy--

190
00:10:04,910 --> 00:10:07,210
so this might be mu,
which could be things

191
00:10:07,210 --> 00:10:12,850
like, how quickly a call
service agent responds

192
00:10:12,850 --> 00:10:16,280
to a calls; how many times
they use positive sentiment,

193
00:10:16,280 --> 00:10:17,530
things like that.

194
00:10:17,530 --> 00:10:19,160
And of course, in
the case of a robot,

195
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:22,130
it might be how many times they
hit a wall, how far it went,

196
00:10:22,130 --> 00:10:24,730
others, any of these
sorts of features.

197
00:10:24,730 --> 00:10:27,010
You could imagine that your
reward function is just

198
00:10:27,010 --> 00:10:30,190
a linear combination
of those features.

199
00:10:30,190 --> 00:10:31,910
And so we saw--

200
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:35,620
So these features are just
things that people can come up

201
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:37,420
with for every problem?

202
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:38,060
Great question.

203
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:40,300
So [INAUDIBLE] asked,
are these features

204
00:10:40,300 --> 00:10:42,350
like people are writing
down per problem?

205
00:10:42,350 --> 00:10:44,350
Historically, yes, I think
one of the big things

206
00:10:44,350 --> 00:10:46,340
with deep learning
has been like,

207
00:10:46,340 --> 00:10:49,130
let's at least go as close
to the sensors as possible.

208
00:10:49,130 --> 00:10:53,440
So can we use just images
instead of features on images?

209
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:56,210
But in the case of something,
like, say, online marketing,

210
00:10:56,210 --> 00:10:58,430
a lot of them would be
potentially predefined.

211
00:10:58,430 --> 00:11:01,750
So purchases and what
web pages you looked at,

212
00:11:01,750 --> 00:11:04,570
and what things you--
search queries you did.

213
00:11:04,570 --> 00:11:06,070
So you would have
to still enumerate

214
00:11:06,070 --> 00:11:07,960
a set of features in
this case that you're

215
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:09,790
defining your reward over.

216
00:11:09,790 --> 00:11:12,310
But ideally, it's sort of
as close to the sensor level

217
00:11:12,310 --> 00:11:14,710
of the data you're
collecting as possible,

218
00:11:14,710 --> 00:11:17,050
or at least that often
has a big advantage.

219
00:11:17,050 --> 00:11:19,420
So what we saw here
is that essentially,

220
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:21,800
because we assume if
things are linear,

221
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:24,140
and we assume there's just
this unknown weight vector--

222
00:11:24,140 --> 00:11:24,980
so this is a vector.

223
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:26,560
This is a vector--

224
00:11:26,560 --> 00:11:28,510
we could say if you
could have, make sure

225
00:11:28,510 --> 00:11:31,180
that your distribution over
features is really close.

226
00:11:31,180 --> 00:11:34,800
And if you bound the norm
of the weight vector,

227
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:36,380
then being really
close in features

228
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:38,960
is the same as being really
close in reward, which

229
00:11:38,960 --> 00:11:41,760
means if your policy can
induce the same features,

230
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:43,310
you can get the same reward.

231
00:11:43,310 --> 00:11:45,140
This is a recap from
last time, but it's

232
00:11:45,140 --> 00:11:47,870
useful to think about
as we go forward.

233
00:11:47,870 --> 00:11:50,120
So one of the big challenges
we talked about last time

234
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:53,510
is that there is not a
unique reward function that

235
00:11:53,510 --> 00:11:55,650
is compatible with
the observed data,

236
00:11:55,650 --> 00:11:59,120
even if you assume your
observed data is optimal.

237
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:01,100
So we talked about
how even the zero

238
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:04,380
reward is compatible with
any policy you might see.

239
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:06,960
And so in general, it's
not can be identifiable.

240
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:09,950
We can't just say, if we
observe these trajectories,

241
00:12:09,950 --> 00:12:11,610
and we know the
policy is optimal,

242
00:12:11,610 --> 00:12:12,960
this is what the reward is.

243
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:15,692
There's too many rewards
that are compatible.

244
00:12:15,692 --> 00:12:17,900
And so what we're going to
spend a lot of time on now

245
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:22,310
is to think about one choice
for how to break that ambiguity.

246
00:12:22,310 --> 00:12:25,920
And this is where we
left off last time.

247
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:33,480
And what we're going to focus
on now is Maximum Entropy IRL.

248
