notice in this case is

994
00:52:05,010 --> 00:52:06,930
when they did this,
they are not claiming

995
00:52:06,930 --> 00:52:10,330
that is actually the reward
model used by people.

996
00:52:10,330 --> 00:52:13,230
It is the reward model that
is compatible with people's

997
00:52:13,230 --> 00:52:16,140
demonstrations that maximizes--

998
00:52:16,140 --> 00:52:19,500
and a distribution
that maximizes entropy.

999
00:52:19,500 --> 00:52:23,220
So it is not necessarily
claiming that it is exactly

1000
00:52:23,220 --> 00:52:26,310
mapping human preferences.

1001
00:52:26,310 --> 00:52:26,830
Awesome.

1002
00:52:26,830 --> 00:52:28,500
So now we're going to get into--

1003
00:52:28,500 --> 00:52:31,020
this is one example
of human feedback

1004
00:52:31,020 --> 00:52:33,870
or human input to trying
to use that to make

1005
00:52:33,870 --> 00:52:36,400
good sequences of decisions
under uncertainty,

1006
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:38,610
but there's actually a huge
number of different ways

1007
00:52:38,610 --> 00:52:39,750
to do this.

1008
00:52:39,750 --> 00:52:42,000
And so now we're going to--
this class and next class,

1009
00:52:42,000 --> 00:52:44,625
we're going to talk some about
human feedback and reinforcement

1010
00:52:44,625 --> 00:52:46,363
learning from human preferences.

1011
00:52:46,363 --> 00:52:49,030
And I think you can think about
this from many different levels.

1012
00:52:49,030 --> 00:52:52,320
You can think about it in terms
of how could humans actively try

1013
00:52:52,320 --> 00:52:54,630
to help reinforcement
learning agents that they

1014
00:52:54,630 --> 00:52:56,250
are trained to do something?

1015
00:52:56,250 --> 00:52:58,440
Like, maybe they want
to train the robot how

1016
00:52:58,440 --> 00:53:00,597
to clean up their
counter in their kitchen,

1017
00:53:00,597 --> 00:53:02,680
and they have a particular
way they want to do it.

1018
00:53:02,680 --> 00:53:04,180
And so they might
be actively trying

1019
00:53:04,180 --> 00:53:06,940
to help an agent do
a particular task;

1020
00:53:06,940 --> 00:53:09,900
or we might be just trying
to align, say, large language

1021
00:53:09,900 --> 00:53:12,810
models with our
values, our intents.

1022
00:53:12,810 --> 00:53:15,570
And so then could we
provide information

1023
00:53:15,570 --> 00:53:19,330
that's going to shape their
behavior across many tasks?

1024
00:53:19,330 --> 00:53:23,770
So it is relevant to both
of these different types

1025
00:53:23,770 --> 00:53:24,662
of objectives.

1026
00:53:24,662 --> 00:53:26,620
And I'm going to go
through some different ways

1027
00:53:26,620 --> 00:53:29,500
that people could be
using human input in terms

1028
00:53:29,500 --> 00:53:32,140
of these sort of training.

1029
00:53:32,140 --> 00:53:34,483
So one thing to
note is that people

1030
00:53:34,483 --> 00:53:36,650
have been thinking about
this for quite a long time.

1031
00:53:36,650 --> 00:53:39,850
I like this work by Andrea
Thomaz and Cynthia Breazeal

1032
00:53:39,850 --> 00:53:41,200
from MIT.

1033
00:53:41,200 --> 00:53:43,550
And they had this thing-- it
looks pretty primitive now,

1034
00:53:43,550 --> 00:53:45,838
but this thing called
Sophie's kitchen.

1035
00:53:45,838 --> 00:53:47,380
And the idea in this
case is that you

1036
00:53:47,380 --> 00:53:50,770
would be trying to teach
an autonomous agent how

1037
00:53:50,770 --> 00:53:53,470
to make a recipe or do
some basic different tasks

1038
00:53:53,470 --> 00:53:54,303
in the kitchen.

1039
00:53:54,303 --> 00:53:55,970
And of course, as you
can see with this,

1040
00:53:55,970 --> 00:53:57,700
we've come a long
way in the last 20

1041
00:53:57,700 --> 00:53:59,230
years, which is wonderful.

1042
00:53:59,230 --> 00:54:02,300
But the kind of key
insight here was, well,

1043
00:54:02,300 --> 00:54:06,610
maybe we could learn much
faster if you have a human.

1044
00:54:06,610 --> 00:54:09,280
Like, instead of having an
agent that's trying out things

1045
00:54:09,280 --> 00:54:11,530
like Epsilon-Greedy and sort
of exploring in the world

1046
00:54:11,530 --> 00:54:15,290
by itself, that's not how
humans do it most of the time.

1047
00:54:15,290 --> 00:54:18,280
Most of the time, we have
things like schools or guardians

1048
00:54:18,280 --> 00:54:21,200
or friends that are giving
us lots of feedback and help

1049
00:54:21,200 --> 00:54:22,950
when we're trying
to learn tasks.

1050
00:54:22,950 --> 00:54:24,990
And so their insight
was to say, well,

1051
00:54:24,990 --> 00:54:27,933
let's try to do more
effective and efficient robot

1052
00:54:27,933 --> 00:54:29,600
learning by leveraging
the fact that you

1053
00:54:29,600 --> 00:54:32,570
can have a human in the loop
that's providing feedback

1054
00:54:32,570 --> 00:54:34,570
to the robot.

1055
00:54:34,570 --> 00:54:37,070
And in this case, I think one
thing that's important to note

1056
00:54:37,070 --> 00:54:39,860
is that the robot is getting
two different forms of input

1057
00:54:39,860 --> 00:54:41,690
that they're trying to maximize.

1058
00:54:41,690 --> 00:54:43,850
They're both getting
input from the human,

1059
00:54:43,850 --> 00:54:46,550
and they're getting input
from the environment.

1060
00:54:46,550 --> 00:54:47,493
So, for example--

1061
00:54:47,493 --> 00:54:50,160
I don't remember if this exactly
was in that particular domain--

1062
00:54:50,160 --> 00:54:52,243
but you could imagine
something like maybe there's

1063
00:54:52,243 --> 00:54:55,650
intrinsic reward if you drop
something like a big cost.

1064
00:54:55,650 --> 00:54:57,980
But then maybe the human
also says that's good

1065
00:54:57,980 --> 00:55:01,040
when you make the right recipe.

1066
00:55:01,040 --> 00:55:04,640
So there's two forms of signals
that are being used to train.

1067
00:55:04,640 --> 00:55:07,230
So this is an example where
it's more like DAGGER.

1068
00:55:07,230 --> 00:55:10,070
You have a human in the loop,
and they are trying actively

1069
00:55:10,070 --> 00:55:13,550
to help the robot all the time.

1070
00:55:13,550 --> 00:55:16,310
Another version of this
is the TAMER framework

1071
00:55:16,310 --> 00:55:20,103
from Brad Knox and Peter
Stone over at UT Austin.

1072
00:55:20,103 --> 00:55:22,020
And what they were,
again, looking at is like,

1073
00:55:22,020 --> 00:55:23,450
well, maybe we can
train agents to do

1074
00:55:23,450 --> 00:55:24,908
things much better
and much quicker

1075
00:55:24,908 --> 00:55:27,380
if we're willing
to be in the loop.

1076
00:55:27,380 --> 00:55:29,210
And these are all
different approaches

1077
00:55:29,210 --> 00:55:30,490
than the DAGGER approach.

1078
00:55:30,490 --> 00:55:33,290
In this approach, so
what are we looking at?

1079
00:55:33,290 --> 00:55:34,500
Again, this was older.

1080
00:55:34,500 --> 00:55:37,140
So this is looking at
Tetris, a video game.

1081
00:55:37,140 --> 00:55:41,070
We're trying to stack
blocks and clear lines.

1082
00:55:41,070 --> 00:55:42,590
And what you could
see in this case

1083
00:55:42,590 --> 00:55:45,480
is a lot of the previous work
like of policy iteration--

1084
00:55:45,480 --> 00:55:47,220
and it doesn't matter exactly
what these algorithms are,

1085
00:55:47,220 --> 00:55:48,500
but there are these sort
of competitive algorithms

1086
00:55:48,500 --> 00:55:49,580
at the time.

1087
00:55:49,580 --> 00:55:51,810
We're, at game three,
getting nothing.

1088
00:55:51,810 --> 00:55:54,560
Like, they just weren't
clearing any lines.

1089
00:55:54,560 --> 00:55:59,220
But after a while, they could
start to learn much better.

1090
00:55:59,220 --> 00:56:01,020
They could get many more later.

1091
00:56:01,020 --> 00:56:04,850
And what they found here is
that by using human feedback,

1092
00:56:04,850 --> 00:56:07,340
they were taking human
feedback, and they were learning

1093
00:56:07,340 --> 00:56:09,288
an explicit reward model.

1094
00:56:09,288 --> 00:56:11,330
So one thing you could
imagine you could be doing

1095
00:56:11,330 --> 00:56:12,930
is doing something
like model-free RL

1096
00:56:12,930 --> 00:56:15,570
where you're getting
signals from the human,

1097
00:56:15,570 --> 00:56:18,545
and you're using that to
update the agent's policy,

1098
00:56:18,545 --> 00:56:19,420
but then you drop it.

1099
00:56:19,420 --> 00:56:22,300
You're not doing any parametric
modeling of the reward model.

1100
00:56:22,300 --> 00:56:25,350
In this case, they are trying to
explicitly build a reward model

1101
00:56:25,350 --> 00:56:26,880
from the human feedback.

1102
00:56:26,880 --> 00:56:28,320
And you could see
that they could

1103
00:56:28,320 --> 00:56:30,690
get much better
performance very quickly.

1104
00:56:30,690 --> 00:56:33,735
But just kind of maybe the
problem with DAGGER, people

1105
00:56:33,735 --> 00:56:35,860
aren't going to stay around
for thousands of games.

1106
00:56:35,860 --> 00:56:38,500
And so you may not be able
to exceed performance,

1107
00:56:38,500 --> 00:56:41,400
at least in this case,
if you allowed the agent

1108
00:56:41,400 --> 00:56:43,765
to train for much longer.

1109
00:56:43,765 --> 00:56:46,140
But I think this is another
example of where-- so this is

1110
00:56:46,140 --> 00:56:47,723
sort of a place where
they're starting

1111
00:56:47,723 --> 00:56:50,040
to do model-based approaches,
where you were actually

1112
00:56:50,040 --> 00:56:53,520
explicitly training a reward
model from human feedback.

1113
00:56:53,520 --> 00:56:56,490
And I think it's nice to think
about there being at least

1114
00:56:56,490 --> 00:56:59,430
one sort of continuum
of the type of support

1115
00:56:59,430 --> 00:57:00,780
that humans could provide--

1116
00:57:00,780 --> 00:57:02,470
really, probably is
multi-dimensional,

1117
00:57:02,470 --> 00:57:05,880
but at least one-- which is, you
could think of-- if humans are

1118
00:57:05,880 --> 00:57:09,160
willing to provide data
at all to train RL agents,

1119
00:57:09,160 --> 00:57:11,580
one might be, I'm only
going to give demonstrations

1120
00:57:11,580 --> 00:57:14,260
that I'm going to do anyway
as part of my normal behavior,

1121
00:57:14,260 --> 00:57:15,930
or maybe that I'll do once.

1122
00:57:15,930 --> 00:57:18,060
And then in another
extreme is something

1123
00:57:18,060 --> 00:57:20,370
like this DAGGER or
this constant teaching,

1124
00:57:20,370 --> 00:57:23,370
where I'm willing to be
a coach for my agent,

1125
00:57:23,370 --> 00:57:26,380
and I'm just going to
sit there the whole time.

1126
00:57:26,380 --> 00:57:28,630
And one of the things you
might wonder is, like, well,

1127
00:57:28,630 --> 00:57:29,380
what's in between.

1128
00:57:29,380 --> 00:57:31,380
This is clearly a spectrum.

1129
00:57:31,380 --> 00:57:34,230
And one thing that
a lot of people

1130
00:57:34,230 --> 00:57:36,480
have thought about quite a
bit over the last 15 years

1131
00:57:36,480 --> 00:57:38,280
is where their preference is.

1132
00:57:38,280 --> 00:57:41,220
Pair comparisons
is that sweet spot.

1133
00:57:41,220 --> 00:57:43,440
So the idea in this
case is that you're not

1134
00:57:43,440 --> 00:57:46,900
going to ask people to
do constant teaching.

1135
00:57:46,900 --> 00:57:48,630
You're not going
to-- but you are

1136
00:57:48,630 --> 00:57:50,647
going to ask them
to do a bit of work.

1137
00:57:50,647 --> 00:57:52,230
And in particular,
you're going to ask

1138
00:57:52,230 --> 00:57:54,940
them to be able to compare
different types of behaviors

1139
00:57:54,940 --> 00:57:57,290
and which do they like better.

1140
00:57:57,290 --> 00:58:00,710
This is kind of in between on
the level of human experts.

1141
00:58:00,710 --> 00:58:02,900
So one of the first places
this was discussed a lot

1142
00:58:02,900 --> 00:58:05,010
was recommendation
ranking systems.

1143
00:58:05,010 --> 00:58:08,330
So Yisong Yue, who's a professor
now at Caltech, together

1144
00:58:08,330 --> 00:58:11,670
with his PhD advisor, Thorsten
Joachims and others at Cornell,

1145
00:58:11,670 --> 00:58:13,610
did some really nice
early work on thinking

1146
00:58:13,610 --> 00:58:16,290
of if you have recommendation
ranking systems.

1147
00:58:16,290 --> 00:58:18,950
So imagine you have two
different retrieval functions,

1148
00:58:18,950 --> 00:58:20,930
and you put in some query.

1149
00:58:20,930 --> 00:58:24,320
And this gives you this-- the
retrieval function A gives you

1150
00:58:24,320 --> 00:58:25,880
this series of outputs.

1151
00:58:25,880 --> 00:58:28,730
And retrieval function
B gives you the other.

1152
00:58:28,730 --> 00:58:33,520
And you'd like to learn because
you are Google or Bing or things

1153
00:58:33,520 --> 00:58:34,020
like that.

1154
00:58:34,020 --> 00:58:37,130
You like to learn which
of these two is better.

1155
00:58:37,130 --> 00:58:39,840
And so the idea that they
came up with in this case is,

1156
00:58:39,840 --> 00:58:42,120
well, we can ask people
which one is better.

1157
00:58:42,120 --> 00:58:44,490
And in particular, you could
ask people to compare, say,

1158
00:58:44,490 --> 00:58:48,470
maybe the first item
returned or the second

1159
00:58:48,470 --> 00:58:51,320
or the complete ranking,
which one is better.

1160
00:58:51,320 --> 00:58:54,080
And that's something that
might be much easier for people

1161
00:58:54,080 --> 00:59:00,020
to do than to specify a scalar
reward function for how good

1162
00:59:00,020 --> 00:59:04,650
it is that CS159 is
returned to your query.

1163
00:59:04,650 --> 00:59:06,500
Is that 17.3?

1164
00:59:06,500 --> 00:59:08,060
Or is that 2,006?

1165
00:59:08,060 --> 00:59:09,840
Or is it minus 7 billion?

1166
00:59:09,840 --> 00:59:12,540
It seems very hard to
ask humans to do that,

1167
00:59:12,540 --> 00:59:14,540
but they probably can
do the comparison.

1168
00:59:14,540 --> 00:59:17,830
And they can say, well, it
seems a little bit better.

1169
00:59:17,830 --> 00:59:18,757
So that's one area.

1170
00:59:18,757 --> 00:59:21,340
And that was sort of one of the
early areas of people thinking

1171
00:59:21,340 --> 00:59:24,253
about how could you get feedback
on recommendation systems

1172
00:59:24,253 --> 00:59:25,670
so that we could
make them better?

1173
00:59:25,670 --> 00:59:27,650
But there are lots of
other applications.

1174
00:59:27,650 --> 00:59:29,560
And as you can see,
robotics and driving

1175
00:59:29,560 --> 00:59:31,370
is one that people have
thought lots about.

1176
00:59:31,370 --> 00:59:33,340
So this is work by Dorsa
Sadigh, another one

1177
00:59:33,340 --> 00:59:34,510
of my great colleagues here.

1178
00:59:34,510 --> 00:59:36,468
And what they were doing
here is to think about

1179
00:59:36,468 --> 00:59:41,680
if you're training a car to have
different behaviors on the road,

1180
00:59:41,680 --> 00:59:44,950
how do you get input from humans
about which types of behaviors

1181
00:59:44,950 --> 00:59:46,750
are going to be acceptable?

1182
00:59:46,750 --> 00:59:49,875
So, for example, most
of us would probably

1183
00:59:49,875 --> 00:59:52,250
prefer the thing on the left
than the thing on the right,

1184
00:59:52,250 --> 00:59:56,050
because the thing on the left
is not involve a car accident.

1185
00:59:56,050 --> 00:59:58,780
But it is hard to write down
an objective function for all

1186
00:59:58,780 --> 01:00:01,450
the things you want to do when
you're driving, including, like,

1187
01:00:01,450 --> 01:00:04,960
if it's hailing, or if a car
suddenly stops in front of you,

1188
01:00:04,960 --> 01:00:07,720
or it's pretty subtle.

1189
01:00:07,720 --> 01:00:10,420
And so what Dorsa and
her colleagues showed

1190
01:00:10,420 --> 01:00:13,460
is that people can do this
sort of preference pairs.

1191
01:00:13,460 --> 01:00:15,603
And in fact, she has
done-- she and her lab here

1192
01:00:15,603 --> 01:00:17,270
has done lots of
really interesting work

1193
01:00:17,270 --> 01:00:20,622
on thinking of which preference
pairs do you show to people so

1194
01:00:20,622 --> 01:00:22,580
that you can quickly try
to get a sense of what

1195
01:00:22,580 --> 01:00:26,450
their preferences are to try
to respect this human effort

1196
01:00:26,450 --> 01:00:27,680
aspect?

1197
01:00:27,680 --> 01:00:30,170
So these are just two of
the examples of the places

1198
01:00:30,170 --> 01:00:32,120
that people have
thought about this.

1199
01:00:32,120 --> 01:00:34,890
And of course,
ChatGPT is another,

1200
01:00:34,890 --> 01:00:37,070
and we'll see more
about that in a second.

1201
01:00:37,070 --> 01:00:38,990
So in general,
pairwise comparisons

1202
01:00:38,990 --> 01:00:41,150
might be in this sweet
spot, because it's

1203
01:00:41,150 --> 01:00:43,980
often easier than writing
down a reward function.

1204
01:00:43,980 --> 01:00:45,950
And it's much less work
than sort of DAGGER

1205
01:00:45,950 --> 01:00:48,140
and having to
constantly say-- you

1206
01:00:48,140 --> 01:00:50,660
could imagine in
recommendation systems.

1207
01:00:50,660 --> 01:00:53,600
What is the perfect
response to this query

1208
01:00:53,600 --> 01:00:55,970
of, I don't know, which
courses involve this?

1209
01:00:55,970 --> 01:00:58,350
That might be really hard
for people to write down,

1210
01:00:58,350 --> 01:01:02,772
but it's easy for them
to do the comparisons.

1211
01:01:02,772 --> 01:01:05,220
Now, how do we think about this?

1212
01:01:05,220 --> 01:01:08,510
Well, one way we could think
about how do we mathematically

1213
01:01:08,510 --> 01:01:11,360
model this is the
Bradley-Terry model.

1214
01:01:11,360 --> 01:01:15,050
And as we've seen with trying
to understand modeling scalar

1215
01:01:15,050 --> 01:01:18,830
rewards, when we start to think
about having people compare

1216
01:01:18,830 --> 01:01:21,020
preferences, often
we will still be

1217
01:01:21,020 --> 01:01:23,990
really interested in
understanding a latent reward

1218
01:01:23,990 --> 01:01:24,860
model.

1219
01:01:24,860 --> 01:01:27,170
So the idea will
often be that we

1220
01:01:27,170 --> 01:01:29,870
assume that people have some
reward function in their head

1221
01:01:29,870 --> 01:01:32,220
that maybe is hard for
them to articulate.

1222
01:01:32,220 --> 01:01:34,760
And what they can do is
produce preference pairs

1223
01:01:34,760 --> 01:01:37,970
that are compatible with that
underlying reward function.

1224
01:01:37,970 --> 01:01:39,210
Now, they might be noisy.

1225
01:01:39,210 --> 01:01:41,245
A lot of us all make mistakes.

1226
01:01:41,245 --> 01:01:42,120
We all make mistakes.

1227
01:01:42,120 --> 01:01:44,180
And so we're not going to
assume that necessarily

1228
01:01:44,180 --> 01:01:46,940
what I say is perfectly
corresponding to my latent

1229
01:01:46,940 --> 01:01:50,690
reward function, but it's going
to be noisly related to that.

1230
01:01:50,690 --> 01:01:53,180
And so Bradley-Terry
model is one

1231
01:01:53,180 --> 01:01:54,860
of these types of
models that tries

1232
01:01:54,860 --> 01:01:59,120
to relate kind of internal
preferences over items

1233
01:01:59,120 --> 01:02:01,593
to how we might compare them.

1234
01:02:01,593 --> 01:02:04,010
All right, so let's first start
off with a simpler setting

1235
01:02:04,010 --> 01:02:06,750
before we get to RL
of K-armed bandits.

1236
01:02:06,750 --> 01:02:10,382
We're going to see K-armed
bandits shortly in the course.

1237
01:02:10,382 --> 01:02:11,840
But for right now,
you don't really

1238
01:02:11,840 --> 01:02:14,460
need to know what it is except
for there's only actions.

1239
01:02:14,460 --> 01:02:16,460
So there's no states
right now, just like you

1240
01:02:16,460 --> 01:02:18,480
have K different actions.

1241
01:02:18,480 --> 01:02:19,260
That's all.

1242
01:02:19,260 --> 01:02:21,500
And they all have
different rewards.

1243
01:02:21,500 --> 01:02:23,210
And what we're
going to assume is

1244
01:02:23,210 --> 01:02:26,150
that a human makes some noisy
pairwise comparisons where

1245
01:02:26,150 --> 01:02:29,130
the probability she
preserves prefers Bi,

1246
01:02:29,130 --> 01:02:34,970
so item B, compared to Bj,
is like the exponential model

1247
01:02:34,970 --> 01:02:35,840
that we saw before.

1248
01:02:35,840 --> 01:02:37,350
Exponential models
come up a lot.

1249
01:02:37,350 --> 01:02:40,400
So it's going to be the
exponential of the reward for Bi

1250
01:02:40,400 --> 01:02:42,020
divided by the
exponential of reward

1251
01:02:42,020 --> 01:02:46,320
for Bi plus the
exponential reward for Bj.

1252
01:02:46,320 --> 01:02:50,280
OK, so what will
be the probability

1253
01:02:50,280 --> 01:02:53,790
that I prefer Bi to Bj if
actually their reward is

1254
01:02:53,790 --> 01:02:55,820
identical to me?

1255
01:02:55,820 --> 01:02:57,820
Actually, I'm like, I
don't mind whether I have,

1256
01:02:57,820 --> 01:03:00,578
I don't know, like deep
dish pizza versus flat.

1257
01:03:00,578 --> 01:03:01,870
I actually do have preferences.

1258
01:03:01,870 --> 01:03:04,090
But imagine that I don't.

1259
01:03:04,090 --> 01:03:07,460
So what would the
probability be in that case,

1260
01:03:07,460 --> 01:03:08,640
according to this model?

1261
01:03:08,640 --> 01:03:11,073
So my internal reward for
both of them is like plus 20

1262
01:03:11,073 --> 01:03:12,240
because I really like pizza.

1263
01:03:12,240 --> 01:03:15,110
So what would that be
for this probability

1264
01:03:15,110 --> 01:03:16,830
if the two items are identical?

1265
01:03:20,710 --> 01:03:21,490
Fisher said.

1266
01:03:21,490 --> 01:03:23,740
Yeah, so this is
automatically normalized.

1267
01:03:23,740 --> 01:03:26,210
So it's 50% at most.

1268
01:03:26,210 --> 01:03:27,728
If I like one thing much more--

1269
01:03:27,728 --> 01:03:29,270
I do like deep dish
pizza a lot more.

1270
01:03:29,270 --> 01:03:34,460
So it's probably more like that
would be, say, 100 versus 10.

1271
01:03:34,460 --> 01:03:37,580
And in that case, my probability
would be more like, say, 0.9

1272
01:03:37,580 --> 01:03:41,890
or 0.95, something like that.

1273
01:03:41,890 --> 01:03:43,820
So this was just a
particular model.

1274
01:03:43,820 --> 01:03:45,070
It is noisy.

1275
01:03:45,070 --> 01:03:46,480
If you read the--

1276
01:03:46,480 --> 01:03:48,940
if I put a link later to
the reinforcement learning

1277
01:03:48,940 --> 01:03:52,150
from human feedback paper, they
make some additional assumptions

1278
01:03:52,150 --> 01:03:56,660
of how people make preference
pairs on top of this model.

1279
01:03:56,660 --> 01:03:58,690
But this is the basic
model that a lot of people

1280
01:03:58,690 --> 01:04:00,640
have been looking at
recently to understand

1281
01:04:00,640 --> 01:04:05,753
how internal latent rewards
relate to external preferences.

1282
01:04:05,753 --> 01:04:07,420
One of the important
things to note here

1283
01:04:07,420 --> 01:04:09,110
is that this model
is transitive,

1284
01:04:09,110 --> 01:04:11,290
which means that
if I want to know

1285
01:04:11,290 --> 01:04:14,620
what this sort of probability
is between i and k--

1286
01:04:14,620 --> 01:04:16,370
so those are two
particular items--

1287
01:04:16,370 --> 01:04:18,970
I can deduce it from
my probability for i

1288
01:04:18,970 --> 01:04:21,110
to j and my probability
from j to k.

1289
01:04:21,110 --> 01:04:22,940
So you can kind of chain things.

1290
01:04:22,940 --> 01:04:26,420
So this is a transitive
probability model.

1291
01:04:26,420 --> 01:04:30,470
So this was introduced
roughly 70 years ago.

1292
01:04:30,470 --> 01:04:33,620
It's a very popular model,
and it came up early

1293
01:04:33,620 --> 01:04:36,470
on in terms of recommendation
systems and others.

1294
01:04:36,470 --> 01:04:38,480
So another thing that's
useful to think about

1295
01:04:38,480 --> 01:04:40,430
is, OK, in this
setting where I just

1296
01:04:40,430 --> 01:04:44,480
have k different actions I can
take, and I want to understand--

1297
01:04:44,480 --> 01:04:46,160
I want to learn
what the reward is

1298
01:04:46,160 --> 01:04:47,840
for somebody, for all of them--

1299
01:04:47,840 --> 01:04:51,380
If I want to think about
finding a maximum one,

1300
01:04:51,380 --> 01:04:54,385
say, like what's the best arm
or what is the best action,

1301
01:04:54,385 --> 01:04:55,760
I might want to
try to understand

1302
01:04:55,760 --> 01:04:57,960
how, under these different
preference models,

1303
01:04:57,960 --> 01:05:00,710
what it means for
something to be good.

1304
01:05:00,710 --> 01:05:02,360
So in the class so
far, we've often

1305
01:05:02,360 --> 01:05:04,700
talked about just maximizing
the value function,

1306
01:05:04,700 --> 01:05:06,930
and we want to find
a policy that's good.

1307
01:05:06,930 --> 01:05:09,260
Now let's just think
about if I have no k

1308
01:05:09,260 --> 01:05:11,300
arms, which of them is best?

1309
01:05:11,300 --> 01:05:15,410
So a Condorcet winner is one
where, for every other item,

1310
01:05:15,410 --> 01:05:19,700
you prefer item i to
all the other items.

1311
01:05:19,700 --> 01:05:21,210
So like, of all
the types of pizza,

1312
01:05:21,210 --> 01:05:24,110
if I like deep dish most,
that's the Condorcet winner.

1313
01:05:24,110 --> 01:05:28,190
And it doesn't mean that it has
to-- that those probabilities

1314
01:05:28,190 --> 01:05:30,950
have to be probably one.

1315
01:05:30,950 --> 01:05:32,430
It just has to be
greater than 0.5.

1316
01:05:32,430 --> 01:05:35,990
It means I have to beat
all of the other options.

1317
01:05:35,990 --> 01:05:37,580
And I'm bringing
these up right now

1318
01:05:37,580 --> 01:05:39,080
because there's
also been some later

1319
01:05:39,080 --> 01:05:43,760
discussion of how does all the
recent RLHF work relate to ideas

1320
01:05:43,760 --> 01:05:47,300
from social choice and
computational economics

1321
01:05:47,300 --> 01:05:49,845
about what are we computing?

1322
01:05:49,845 --> 01:05:52,220
What is the sort of underlying
objective we're computing,

1323
01:05:52,220 --> 01:05:55,670
and how are we distinguishing
between different sorts

1324
01:05:55,670 --> 01:05:58,220
of responses LLMs could give us?

1325
01:05:58,220 --> 01:06:00,500
So the second thing-- so
this is a pretty high bar.

1326
01:06:00,500 --> 01:06:03,110
This means that there
has to be one thing that

1327
01:06:03,110 --> 01:06:05,360
beats everything else.

1328
01:06:05,360 --> 01:06:08,100
A Copelan winner is
a little bit less.

1329
01:06:08,100 --> 01:06:10,760
It just says it's
the winner if it

1330
01:06:10,760 --> 01:06:12,650
has the highest number
of pairwise victories

1331
01:06:12,650 --> 01:06:14,120
against everything else.

1332
01:06:14,120 --> 01:06:17,000
So that doesn't mean that
you have to prefer it

1333
01:06:17,000 --> 01:06:18,480
to everything else.

1334
01:06:18,480 --> 01:06:22,345
It just that means, on
average, it beats the others.

1335
01:06:24,860 --> 01:06:27,120
And a Borda winner--

1336
01:06:27,120 --> 01:06:29,300
an item is a border
winner, if it

1337
01:06:29,300 --> 01:06:33,050
maximizes the expected score,
where the score for item Bj

1338
01:06:33,050 --> 01:06:34,010
is 1.

1339
01:06:34,010 --> 01:06:39,300
If you prefer Bi to Bj,
it's 0.5 if they're equal,

1340
01:06:39,300 --> 01:06:40,560
then it's 0 otherwise.

1341
01:06:40,560 --> 01:06:42,920
So it's sort of like this
discretization of the wins

1342
01:06:42,920 --> 01:06:44,790
and loses.

1343
01:06:44,790 --> 01:06:48,287
And typically, algorithms for
K-armed or dueling bandits--

1344
01:06:48,287 --> 01:06:50,370
again, we'll go into what
bandits are more later--

1345
01:06:50,370 --> 01:06:53,580
what they focus on doing
is trying to find this.

1346
01:06:53,580 --> 01:06:56,230
I don't necessarily
need to find an item,

1347
01:06:56,230 --> 01:06:58,660
say, like, a ranking
system that is always

1348
01:06:58,660 --> 01:07:00,860
better than everything else.

1349
01:07:00,860 --> 01:07:03,337
I want to find one that on
average beats everything else,

1350
01:07:03,337 --> 01:07:05,920
and they often would construct
these kind of pairwise matrices

1351
01:07:05,920 --> 01:07:07,900
where you can think do
these different actions,

1352
01:07:07,900 --> 01:07:08,942
beat these other actions.

1353
01:07:11,800 --> 01:07:13,630
All right, so how
would we learn these?

1354
01:07:13,630 --> 01:07:18,490
So the question, is we have
all of these noisy pairwise

1355
01:07:18,490 --> 01:07:19,300
comparisons.

1356
01:07:19,300 --> 01:07:20,800
And what we want
to do now is to see

1357
01:07:20,800 --> 01:07:24,340
if we can extract these
underlying reward functions.

1358
01:07:24,340 --> 01:07:25,700
Why would we want to do that?

1359
01:07:25,700 --> 01:07:27,908
Well, once we have these
underlying reward functions,

1360
01:07:27,908 --> 01:07:30,530
we can figure out which arm is
best or which action is best.

1361
01:07:30,530 --> 01:07:32,155
And in the reinforcement
learning case,

1362
01:07:32,155 --> 01:07:35,200
we could try to optimize
for that reward function.

1363
01:07:35,200 --> 01:07:36,075
So how do we do that?

1364
01:07:36,075 --> 01:07:37,617
What we're going to
do is we're going

1365
01:07:37,617 --> 01:07:39,950
to assume we have N tuples
of the following form.

1366
01:07:39,950 --> 01:07:44,080
We have item 1, item 2,
So item i, item j, and mu,

1367
01:07:44,080 --> 01:07:46,810
where mu is 1 if you
prefer the first thing.

1368
01:07:46,810 --> 01:07:49,630
Mu is 0 if you prefer
the other thing.

1369
01:07:49,630 --> 01:07:51,940
And it's 0.5 if you don't care.

1370
01:07:51,940 --> 01:07:54,580
So this is just like
a classification task.

1371
01:07:54,580 --> 01:07:56,740
You can just think back to
your supervised learning

1372
01:07:56,740 --> 01:07:58,870
where you just
have-- it should look

1373
01:07:58,870 --> 01:08:02,080
very much like a
logistic regression task.

1374
01:08:02,080 --> 01:08:07,500
And you can maximize the
likelihood with cross-entropy.

1375
01:08:07,500 --> 01:08:11,910
So we had to map it back
to a standard logistic loss

1376
01:08:11,910 --> 01:08:14,428
where we say these
reward models--

1377
01:08:14,428 --> 01:08:16,470
and in general, we're
going to parameterize these

1378
01:08:16,470 --> 01:08:19,830
as like deep neural networks or
some other complicated function.

1379
01:08:19,830 --> 01:08:20,729
It could be linear.

1380
01:08:20,729 --> 01:08:22,229
It just depends.

1381
01:08:22,229 --> 01:08:24,479
But once we have
that, then we can

1382
01:08:24,479 --> 01:08:27,840
try to find the
set of parameters

1383
01:08:27,840 --> 01:08:31,050
that maximize the likelihood.

1384
01:08:31,050 --> 01:08:32,520
So that's how we
could fit a reward

1385
01:08:32,520 --> 01:08:35,670
model when we are
given preference pairs

1386
01:08:35,670 --> 01:08:38,229
and observed preferences.

1387
01:08:38,229 --> 01:08:40,430
Now, you might wonder,
how do we do this in RL?

1388
01:08:40,430 --> 01:08:41,870
Because in RL, we have states.

1389
01:08:41,870 --> 01:08:43,310
We have multiple actions.

1390
01:08:43,310 --> 01:08:44,800
We have trajectories.

1391
01:08:44,800 --> 01:08:47,859
The idea is pretty similar
in some ways to what we

1392
01:08:47,859 --> 01:08:49,180
were seeing with max entropy.

1393
01:08:49,180 --> 01:08:50,930
And that what we're
going to do is to say,

1394
01:08:50,930 --> 01:08:52,040
well, we have a trajectory.

1395
01:08:52,040 --> 01:08:53,957
If we have a trajectory,
we can think of there

1396
01:08:53,957 --> 01:08:56,109
being a series of rewards.

1397
01:08:56,109 --> 01:09:00,010
So the reward of that
trajectory is just the sum.

1398
01:09:00,010 --> 01:09:02,439
So I plug in all of those sums.

1399
01:09:02,439 --> 01:09:07,060
And I prefer a trajectory
if I can get higher reward

1400
01:09:07,060 --> 01:09:08,859
for that trajectory
than the other,

1401
01:09:08,859 --> 01:09:10,359
according to the same model.

1402
01:09:10,359 --> 01:09:12,100
So I essentially just
map it back to as

1403
01:09:12,100 --> 01:09:15,250
if it was kind of like a
bandit, just now that I have

1404
01:09:15,250 --> 01:09:16,810
two different trajectories.

1405
01:09:16,810 --> 01:09:20,240
And we'll see an example
of this in just a minute.

1406
01:09:20,240 --> 01:09:22,420
OK, so what do we do?

1407
01:09:22,420 --> 01:09:25,660
We now are going to ask people
to compare trajectories.

1408
01:09:25,660 --> 01:09:28,782
We'll use that, then we're
going to learn our reward model.

1409
01:09:28,782 --> 01:09:30,740
And then once we have
our learned reward model,

1410
01:09:30,740 --> 01:09:33,040
we can do PPO or
something with it.

1411
01:09:33,040 --> 01:09:35,540
So this gives us a reward
model for our domain,

1412
01:09:35,540 --> 01:09:39,100
and now we can try to optimize
our policy with respect to it.

1413
01:09:39,100 --> 01:09:42,870
So let's look at an example.

1414
01:09:42,870 --> 01:09:45,590
So in the reinforcement
learning from human feedback,

1415
01:09:45,590 --> 01:09:48,070
more precisely called deep
RL from human preferences--

1416
01:09:48,070 --> 01:09:50,180
this came out in 2017.

1417
01:09:50,180 --> 01:09:54,260
And they wanted to train
something to do a backflip.

1418
01:09:54,260 --> 01:09:56,210
And what they
noticed here is they

1419
01:09:56,210 --> 01:09:58,520
needed about 900 bits
of human feedback

1420
01:09:58,520 --> 01:10:00,420
in order to learn to do this.

1421
01:10:00,420 --> 01:10:03,060
So let's see what it looks like.

1422
01:10:03,060 --> 01:10:05,150
All right.

1423
01:10:05,150 --> 01:10:07,400
OK, so what someone's going
to be doing in this case--

1424
01:10:07,400 --> 01:10:08,900
so remember, they're
trying to train

1425
01:10:08,900 --> 01:10:13,090
this sort of little MuJoCo-like
thing to do a backflip.

1426
01:10:13,090 --> 01:10:14,590
So what they're
going to show people

1427
01:10:14,590 --> 01:10:16,630
is they're going to
show them little clips.

1428
01:10:16,630 --> 01:10:18,733
And they're going to say,
is the thing on the left

1429
01:10:18,733 --> 01:10:20,650
doing a better job of
trying to do a backflip,

1430
01:10:20,650 --> 01:10:22,060
or is the thing on the right?

1431
01:10:22,060 --> 01:10:23,727
And they're just
getting people to click

1432
01:10:23,727 --> 01:10:25,870
left, right, left, right,
left, right, left, right.

1433
01:10:25,870 --> 01:10:28,090
And so they're not having
to say what is the reward

1434
01:10:28,090 --> 01:10:29,460
function for doing a backflip?

1435
01:10:29,460 --> 01:10:30,880
They're just saying,
I don't know,

1436
01:10:30,880 --> 01:10:35,461
this one looks closer
to a backflip or better.

1437
01:10:35,461 --> 01:10:38,350
And so what you can see
here is that some of them

1438
01:10:38,350 --> 01:10:40,270
are going to be much
better at getting

1439
01:10:40,270 --> 01:10:41,660
close to doing a back flip.

1440
01:10:41,660 --> 01:10:43,750
So some of those is
actually pretty good.

1441
01:10:43,750 --> 01:10:46,030
And what they are saying
is that they only needed

1442
01:10:46,030 --> 01:10:50,260
about 900 examples
in order to train it

1443
01:10:50,260 --> 01:10:53,440
so that it could actually
learn to do a backflip, which

1444
01:10:53,440 --> 01:10:56,260
is pretty good, particularly
if you think back

1445
01:10:56,260 --> 01:11:00,790
to deep Q-learning and the
enormous amount of data

1446
01:11:00,790 --> 01:11:03,550
and training that
they're often doing for,

1447
01:11:03,550 --> 01:11:05,218
say, trying to learn
Atari, et cetera,

1448
01:11:05,218 --> 01:11:06,385
which is literally millions.

1449
01:11:09,910 --> 01:11:11,220
So this is really cool.

1450
01:11:11,220 --> 01:11:12,285
This is possible to do.

1451
01:11:12,285 --> 01:11:13,910
And this is something
that you're going

1452
01:11:13,910 --> 01:11:15,118
to be doing in your homework.

1453
01:11:15,118 --> 01:11:17,987
So in homework 3, you're going
to be doing both RLHF and DPO.

1454
01:11:17,987 --> 01:11:19,820
So I'm really excited
about this assignment.

1455
01:11:19,820 --> 01:11:21,403
This is the first
time we're doing it.

1456
01:11:21,403 --> 01:11:23,320
So you can actually
see how this works.

1457
01:11:23,320 --> 01:11:25,070
So you can see how
we can actually

1458
01:11:25,070 --> 01:11:26,643
learn from human preferences.

1459
01:11:26,643 --> 01:11:28,560
We're not making you do
the human preferences.

1460
01:11:28,560 --> 01:11:32,030
We're going to give you a data
set for how we can actually

1461
01:11:32,030 --> 01:11:33,530
train these agents.

1462
01:11:33,530 --> 01:11:35,690
Now, I know we're
almost out of time,

1463
01:11:35,690 --> 01:11:37,770
but I'll say just a
little bit about this.

1464
01:11:37,770 --> 01:11:40,010
I'll probably have a
bit of time on Monday

1465
01:11:40,010 --> 01:11:42,533
before we have
our guest lecture.

1466
01:11:42,533 --> 01:11:44,700
But just I want to give you
at least a little taste.

1467
01:11:44,700 --> 01:11:48,410
So this was in 2017,
that paper was.

1468
01:11:48,410 --> 01:11:50,850
And there was attention to it.

1469
01:11:50,850 --> 01:11:53,060
But I feel like in
many ways, there

1470
01:11:53,060 --> 01:11:55,750
wasn't a huge amount of work on
that until much more recently.

1471
01:11:55,750 --> 01:11:57,500
So I just want to share
