So if we just think back--

1474
01:12:03,120 --> 01:12:06,320
I think I showed this slide on
the very first day of class--

1475
01:12:06,320 --> 01:12:09,740
how is RLHF being
used for ChatGPT?

1476
01:12:09,740 --> 01:12:11,300
What they're doing
there is they're

1477
01:12:11,300 --> 01:12:12,925
getting demonstration
data, and they're

1478
01:12:12,925 --> 01:12:14,150
doing supervised learning.

1479
01:12:14,150 --> 01:12:16,733
This is basically what we
would call behavior cloning.

1480
01:12:16,733 --> 01:12:18,650
Then they're going to
get this comparison data

1481
01:12:18,650 --> 01:12:19,980
and train a reward model.

1482
01:12:19,980 --> 01:12:22,320
Now, in their case, they
might not just use two.

1483
01:12:22,320 --> 01:12:25,380
You could actually have
people rank between, say,

1484
01:12:25,380 --> 01:12:26,670
four or something like that.

1485
01:12:26,670 --> 01:12:28,580
And you can extend
these models to do that.

1486
01:12:28,580 --> 01:12:33,560
So you get labelers to do that,
then you train the reward model.

1487
01:12:33,560 --> 01:12:41,510
And then use PPO to actually
update the large language model.

1488
01:12:41,510 --> 01:12:44,150
Now, one thing that I think is
important to note in this case

1489
01:12:44,150 --> 01:12:47,090
is that this is all really an
instance of meta reinforcement

1490
01:12:47,090 --> 01:12:49,220
learning in the sense
that what they're

1491
01:12:49,220 --> 01:12:50,670
going to be trying to do here--

1492
01:12:50,670 --> 01:12:52,520
unlike where we've
seen like you want

1493
01:12:52,520 --> 01:12:54,440
to train something
to do one task,

1494
01:12:54,440 --> 01:12:56,865
like, being able
to do a backflip--

1495
01:12:56,865 --> 01:12:59,240
they're trying to learn in
general a reward function that

1496
01:12:59,240 --> 01:13:01,580
covers all the tasks that
people might want to do

1497
01:13:01,580 --> 01:13:03,380
with large language models.

1498
01:13:03,380 --> 01:13:06,200
And so it's this
multitask problem.

1499
01:13:06,200 --> 01:13:07,820
And so when they
do this, they're

1500
01:13:07,820 --> 01:13:11,360
going to give you a new prompt,
like, write a story about frogs.

1501
01:13:11,360 --> 01:13:13,370
And then they will
want the agent

1502
01:13:13,370 --> 01:13:15,830
to do well on that, which is
likely a task that has maybe

1503
01:13:15,830 --> 01:13:17,842
never seen before in its data.

1504
01:13:17,842 --> 01:13:20,300
So I think that's also another
important thing to note here

1505
01:13:20,300 --> 01:13:22,760
is that the reward models
that are being trained now

1506
01:13:22,760 --> 01:13:26,120
are things that probably would
have been considered multitask

1507
01:13:26,120 --> 01:13:27,240
settings before.

1508
01:13:27,240 --> 01:13:29,420
But now we're sort of
lifting them and saying

1509
01:13:29,420 --> 01:13:31,580
your task is just to
do whatever humans

1510
01:13:31,580 --> 01:13:35,900
want to do with this ChatGPT in
terms of answering questions.

1511
01:13:35,900 --> 01:13:37,790
And so how do you train
a reward model that

1512
01:13:37,790 --> 01:13:40,310
will be good for any of those?

1513
01:13:40,310 --> 01:13:42,420
So we'll continue talking
about this next week.

1514
01:13:42,420 --> 01:13:44,660
We'll talk probably either
before or after the guest

1515
01:13:44,660 --> 01:13:47,930
lecture a bit about how
we actually do this.

1516
01:13:47,930 --> 01:13:50,330
But it basically just follows
exactly along the framework

1517
01:13:50,330 --> 01:13:51,740
that we've just seen there.

1518
01:13:51,740 --> 01:13:52,870
And I'll see you then.

1519
01:13:52,870 --> 01:13:54,420
Thanks.

